1
00:00:29,560 --> 00:01:47,160
Thank you very much, Bill. And I'm delighted to hear that free enterprise will survive till 2015. That's one of the more optimistic forecasts I've heard. And I mean it.

2
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:54,260
Let me say I recently had reason to go and dig

3
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:58,220
into the social literature around the time World War II

4
00:01:58,220 --> 00:01:59,760
broke out.

5
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:04,240
And there were very few people in any country

6
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:08,780
who thought it at all remotely possible

7
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:12,760
that free enterprise could survive a world war,

8
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:18,220
let alone the inevitable post-war depression.

9
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:22,820
And as you know, we haven't done so badly.

10
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:25,200
And so it led me to thinking, to question,

11
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:33,500
what explains that these last 45 years in whatever

12
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:38,920
part of the world the communists didn't manage to take over,

13
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:43,820
by and large, moved into a free market or a market

14
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:48,480
economy with a minimum of restrictions

15
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:52,980
on the movement of capital, some substantial restriction

16
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:56,040
on the movement of goods, and very severe ones

17
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:57,840
on the movement of people.

18
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:02,560
But capital, by and large, is moving very freely.

19
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:07,200
And with, certainly in developed countries,

20
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:11,160
a freedom of access that was unknown,

21
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:16,300
not only in the interwar years, but when you go back,

22
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:23,790
really by and large even before world war one uh and one answer is of course performance

23
00:03:24,590 --> 00:03:35,730
uh maybe the decisive turning point was when a german economist who found himself

24
00:03:36,370 --> 00:03:45,790
minister of economics under the american occupation defied his american masters and in 1949

25
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:55,260
overnight removed all government regulations and restrictions and currency control and rationing

26
00:03:55,820 --> 00:04:01,660
in a still starving and impoverished germany and released the

27
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:12,060
german economic miracle and another turning point came two years later maybe three years later

28
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:34,260
When in an occupied Germany, occupied Japan, a Japanese civil servant, whose name was Yoshida, defied his American masters who were totally, don't believe, said we were the bearers of free enterprise.

29
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:49,160
In those years, we were the bearers of controlled, planned, government-regulated economies, in part because the then administration we had solved its internal problem.

30
00:04:50,290 --> 00:05:03,040
It could not get through the measures the left wing of the Democrats wanted in economics, and yet it could not get rid of them.

31
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:12,240
So it solved the problem by shipping them into power positions in the occupied countries of Germany and Japan.

32
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:21,240
And they there tried to legislate what the American Congress wasn't willing to accept, and Japan particularly.

33
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:27,560
And here was a Japanese bureaucrat who defied his American masters.

34
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:50,680
And in 1952, even before the peace treaty was signed, basically removed, not government control, that's the last thing a Japanese would do, but turned around the way government control was exercised as a restraint on business to a support for business.

35
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:56,940
And he, MacArthur, let me say, the Korean War saved that man.

36
00:05:57,080 --> 00:06:03,700
MacArthur was so angry at him and at this defiance of what everybody knew

37
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:08,380
were the immutable laws of history that he asked for his head.

38
00:06:08,780 --> 00:06:11,940
And the Korean War broke out and MacArthur was in Korea

39
00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:15,460
and too busy to get rid of Mr. Yoshida.

40
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:18,840
Maybe those were the two crucial events.

41
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:40,620
And from there on, there is performance, and instead of the post-war collapse, which everybody knew was inevitable, and the 35% unemployment, we got the fastest and quickest and most comprehensive expansion of the economy, the world economy, we have ever seen.

42
00:06:40,620 --> 00:06:56,740
But I think it is clear now that we only postpone the question of the structure of an advanced

43
00:06:56,740 --> 00:06:58,500
industrial economy.

44
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:00,400
We did not solve it.

45
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:07,780
I know this is not what the people in power in Washington today believe, but I think the

46
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:14,380
fact that they are really now batting their heads against the wall is in large measure

47
00:07:14,380 --> 00:07:22,380
the result of their deluding themselves that we have the answers and they're obvious and they're

48
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:31,280
generally accepted and we are facing a crisis because of our success and when you look at what

49
00:07:31,280 --> 00:07:41,210
is the crisis it is basically a crisis of legitimacy and it's most pronounced in the

50
00:07:41,210 --> 00:07:52,030
leading country that is this country uh in 1950 or so when we were flush with the first uh signs of

