But they are also the managers of their pension fund,

221
00:27:03,050 --> 00:27:07,750
which owns 21% of the stock.

222
00:27:07,750 --> 00:27:15,170
And here is Harper and Rowe selling at the stock exchange for $24 a share.

223
00:27:15,970 --> 00:27:18,390
And Mr. Murdoch offers you $65.

224
00:27:21,380 --> 00:27:23,580
Just assume they had said no.

225
00:27:24,440 --> 00:27:28,920
And six months later, the Harper and Rowe share would sell at $24,

226
00:27:30,190 --> 00:27:37,760
which is all it can possibly justify on the basis of present and potential earnings.

227
00:27:38,440 --> 00:27:41,560
What Murdoch expects to get out of it, I don't know.

228
00:27:41,620 --> 00:27:48,350
but it's his money, not mine, but other than prestige.

229
00:27:49,710 --> 00:27:55,050
And he sells the 24, and then any employee of Harper & Voe,

230
00:27:55,310 --> 00:27:56,550
and then the outside shareholders,

231
00:27:57,400 --> 00:28:02,340
could have sued a man, the trustees, for dereliction of duty

232
00:28:02,340 --> 00:28:04,840
and would have won triple damages.

233
00:28:05,320 --> 00:28:07,520
This is a breach of fiduciary duty.

234
00:28:07,920 --> 00:28:10,600
But that only aggravates it. It isn't the cause.

235
00:28:10,600 --> 00:28:29,750
And the reason for this is that fundamentally we have created, we have eliminated ownership without a substitute for it.

236
00:28:29,850 --> 00:28:37,500
Because a pension fund isn't an owner, it's barely an investor, it's a speculator, and has to be.

237
00:28:38,760 --> 00:28:41,480
And yet it has the title of ownership.

238
00:28:43,370 --> 00:28:47,530
And on the other hand, we have eliminated accountability of management.

239
00:28:47,530 --> 00:29:00,810
It turns out that the old political theory that one has to be accountable for something to somebody,

240
00:29:02,140 --> 00:29:11,660
or else one is a despot and illegitimate, that still holds, and that good intentions are no substitute, therefore.

241
00:29:11,660 --> 00:29:28,890
And so with the hostile takeover simply spotlights, highlights, a crisis of, call it constitutional crisis, of basic concepts, created by success.

242
00:29:29,310 --> 00:29:30,290
It's always the toughest.

243
00:29:33,900 --> 00:29:41,000
Now, we do know, let me say, I think one can say that the hostile takeover is a thing of the past.

244
00:29:41,000 --> 00:30:02,360
Not only because we are going to make it increasingly difficult legally, but also because it rested on asset values not having been adjusted to inflation.

245
00:30:03,040 --> 00:30:05,120
And that adjustment is largely over.

246
00:30:05,960 --> 00:30:07,560
There are no pickings anymore.

247
00:30:07,560 --> 00:30:20,660
All that's left is what Murdoch did last month to pay 65 bucks for something that perhaps you can squeeze it up to where it's worth 30.

248
00:30:21,020 --> 00:30:23,600
But that's all you can possibly do.

249
00:30:23,880 --> 00:30:37,320
There isn't, in a very competitive industry with tremendous overcapacity and very poor distribution, there is no way for the leaders to improve their performance.

250
00:30:37,320 --> 00:30:47,040
You can only take it away from each other, and as all of you have learned, you can only take it away from each other if you sacrifice profit.

251
00:30:47,850 --> 00:30:50,790
So that's all there is in this industry. That's all that's left.

252
00:30:52,350 --> 00:30:58,190
And those two things will, I think, very fast put an end to the hostile takeover,

253
00:30:59,280 --> 00:31:02,940
including also the fact that most of the existing

254
00:31:02,940 --> 00:31:09,860
heritages of the inflationary period

255
00:31:09,860 --> 00:31:12,320
have by now been liquidated.

256
00:31:13,040 --> 00:31:16,220
Most of the companies that diversified

257
00:31:16,220 --> 00:31:19,700
because they didn't know what to do with their cash

258
00:31:19,700 --> 00:31:22,800
and it lost value every day

259
00:31:22,800 --> 00:31:26,000
have by now divested and spun off

260
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:28,300
the great bulk of those things.

261
00:31:28,300 --> 00:31:31,200
They have all learned that they don't know how to manage them.

262
00:31:32,230 --> 00:31:34,870
One of the wholesome lessons of the last few years

263
00:31:34,870 --> 00:31:42,180
is that you can't manage businesses by reports and good intentions.

264
00:31:42,600 --> 00:31:44,400
You have to know what you are doing.

265
00:31:50,590 --> 00:31:54,450
And to know what you are doing, you have to understand the business.

266
00:31:55,770 --> 00:31:58,430
And we have learned the lesson again.

267
00:31:58,870 --> 00:32:01,190
Mind you, this is the third time in my life

268
00:32:01,190 --> 00:32:07,160
because I came in as a boy in the aftermath of the European inflations,

269
00:32:09,440 --> 00:32:14,980
and my father, who was the leading Austrian government servant in the economic sphere,

270
00:32:15,440 --> 00:32:18,680
was asked by the Austrian government to leave the government

271
00:32:18,680 --> 00:32:26,480
and take over the chairmanship of a bank to liquidate the stranded post-inflation Wales.

272
00:32:27,290 --> 00:32:32,830
So for three years I heard nothing else around the dinner table when I was 13 or so.

273
00:32:32,830 --> 00:32:51,210
And then I came into banking in the 30s and made a respectable living and earned a lot of money for my employer by liquidating the stranded whales, such as insult, of the inflation of the 20s.

274
00:32:51,210 --> 00:32:56,990
And then one learns that managements have to know what they are doing, or they will

275
00:32:56,990 --> 00:32:59,450
only make the bankers rich.

276
00:32:59,450 --> 00:33:04,060
And will you please accept the fact that they don't need us?

277
00:33:04,060 --> 00:33:10,290
Lawyers, accountants, and bankers have a much more powerful patron.

278
00:33:10,290 --> 00:33:12,030
They have Uncle Sam.

279
00:33:12,030 --> 00:33:14,910
They don't need us.

280
00:33:14,910 --> 00:33:20,550
And so we are almost at the end of the liquidation cycle.

281
00:33:20,550 --> 00:33:27,080
Most of those extravaganzas have faced up to reality,

282
00:33:27,080 --> 00:33:30,070
and there are still a few around.

283
00:33:30,070 --> 00:33:35,100
Anybody wants to liquidate USX, I

284
00:33:35,100 --> 00:33:38,210
think that's going to make somebody rich,

285
00:33:38,210 --> 00:33:39,770
but it's going to be very hard work.

286
00:33:39,770 --> 00:33:44,220
I don't know whether life is worthwhile.

287
00:33:44,220 --> 00:33:48,840
But there are a few around, misbegotten ones that quite

288
00:33:48,840 --> 00:33:51,840
clearly cannot possibly work.

289
00:33:51,840 --> 00:33:53,340
But not very many.

290
00:33:53,340 --> 00:34:00,700
most of them are gone or going and so the hustle takeover is over but the problem is there

291
00:34:01,340 --> 00:34:09,210
the problems because we have two and let me not talk today about the pension fund and how we

292
00:34:09,770 --> 00:34:23,680
can make it be run for the benefit of its two beneficiaries because that's its problem

293
00:34:23,680 --> 00:34:32,320
yeah on the one hand the law says unmistakably that the pension fund trustees have one fiduciary

294
00:34:32,320 --> 00:34:40,440
duty to the future beneficiary and at the same time the law equally unmistakably says that in

295
00:34:40,440 --> 00:34:50,590
a defined benefit fund the management that put in the money is the boss and simply because the

296
00:34:50,590 --> 00:35:00,350
results of the pension fund immediately affect the results of the company for whom the pension

297
00:35:00,350 --> 00:35:10,720
fund is a trustee and those two are incompatible and we tried to point this out in 1950-52 in a

298
00:35:10,720 --> 00:35:21,360
series of learned legal papers and nobody listened and it's pretty obvious and how we're going to

299
00:35:21,360 --> 00:35:29,530
solve it. I have my prejudices, but I don't know the answer. But it's going to be probably solved

300
00:35:29,530 --> 00:35:40,960
by the defined benefit plan, disappearing within two years if we have a stock market down there,

301
00:35:40,960 --> 00:35:47,120
because it's going to be too expensive. And believe me, there's one thing about the stock

302
00:35:47,120 --> 00:35:57,660
market old JP Morgan who ought to have known was asked by a bright young man what he could say

303
00:35:57,660 --> 00:36:03,740
about the stock market he said there's only one thing young man to need to know about the stock

304
00:36:03,740 --> 00:36:18,140
market eventually it will always go down and so that is wisdom and when it does this for 18 months

305
00:36:18,140 --> 00:36:27,240
then most of our defined benefit plan will have been either dissolved or converted into a defined contribution plan,

306
00:36:27,540 --> 00:36:31,960
and we will have solved that problem, not ideally, but somewhat,

307
00:36:31,960 --> 00:36:39,400
and have given up, however, in the process the great hope of 30 years ago

308
00:36:39,400 --> 00:36:44,320
that the pension plan would solve the social problem of the 19th century.

309
00:36:44,320 --> 00:36:52,060
the incompatibility of interest between the employer, the enterprise, not the

310
00:36:52,060 --> 00:37:00,000
capital and the employee. The capital is that yesterday, the enterprise is today

311
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:06,500
and I think we will forget and that we leave the question of the accountability

312
00:37:06,500 --> 00:37:15,500
of management and let me say we know for what but we don't know to whom. For what is

313
00:37:15,500 --> 00:37:23,010
reasonably easy. We know how one makes that balance by building into the system

314
00:37:23,010 --> 00:37:30,540
the yardsticks and measurements that go beyond the immediate short term

315
00:37:30,540 --> 00:37:38,690
bottom line. We know, and I hope everybody in this room knows it, that the short

316
00:37:38,690 --> 00:37:49,140
term bottom line is capable of infinite manipulation. I hope I'm not the only old

317
00:37:49,140 --> 00:37:56,660
accountant in here but any old accountant knows that the old story that ends with the words of the

318
00:37:56,660 --> 00:38:03,460
you know the fellow looking for a controller the one who got the job was telling me what you needed

319
00:38:03,460 --> 00:38:11,680
to be the question was how much is two and two and the one who said tell me how much you needed

320
00:38:11,680 --> 00:38:23,650
to be got the job he was the accountant the other one was a statistician said two and two is 3.9

321
00:38:23,970 --> 00:38:27,030
with 80% probability.

322
00:38:27,030 --> 00:38:31,330
And then there was a long-range planner.

323
00:38:31,330 --> 00:38:33,070
But the accountant was the one who

324
00:38:33,070 --> 00:38:38,870
says, tell me how much you need it to be over the short term.

325
00:38:38,870 --> 00:38:45,190
The bottom line is that it's not a very dependable measurement

326
00:38:45,190 --> 00:38:47,470
of anything.

327
00:38:47,470 --> 00:38:51,490
And that's why old financial people like me

328
00:38:51,490 --> 00:38:56,530
start reading an annual report from the back where

329
00:38:56,530 --> 00:39:07,800
flow of funds statement is. That's not so easy to fake. It's a necessary, but by no means a

330
00:39:07,800 --> 00:39:16,790
conclusive measurement. And we know that market standing, performance as innovator, and

331
00:39:16,790 --> 00:39:24,210
productivities are the ones in which short range and short and long range are balanced. And

332
00:39:24,210 --> 00:39:33,300
And therefore, if you build them into your system, you have reasonably reliable guideposts.

333
00:39:33,300 --> 00:39:40,070
So we know what management should be accountable for, but to whom and how.

334
00:39:40,070 --> 00:39:47,580
For a long time, a good many of us have been preaching that you need an effective board,

335
00:39:48,990 --> 00:39:51,010
and a few of you have worked on it.

336
00:39:52,930 --> 00:39:55,690
And the ones who have worked on it are better off.

337
00:39:55,690 --> 00:40:07,850
He says so far an effective board has been the only defense, real defense, against the hostile takeover because they can mobilize the community.

338
00:40:09,730 --> 00:40:11,610
But it is not adequate.

339
00:40:11,610 --> 00:40:36,200
And how do you build accountability into whom for the survival of the enterprise there's a growing concern, which is also the most, gives you the highest return on the investment.

340
00:40:38,120 --> 00:40:49,650
You all have heard that nine out of ten of companies that have been taken over do less well a year after than they did when they were taken over.

341
00:40:49,650 --> 00:40:51,670
The figure is closer to 100.

342
00:40:52,350 --> 00:40:55,630
There are almost no exceptions of a hostile takeover

343
00:40:55,630 --> 00:41:02,170
because the hostile takeover has to run the business for the very short term

344
00:41:02,170 --> 00:41:09,880
so that the takeover artist can repay himself and pay off the bank loan.

345
00:41:11,000 --> 00:41:18,150
And then he has to milk the business, and within a year, profits go down.

346
00:41:18,150 --> 00:41:30,790
So, we know that this is not the road to give the shareholder optimum or maximum returns,

347
00:41:30,790 --> 00:41:36,850
even though if you leave out the short term, you also rob the shareholder, but you rob

348
00:41:36,850 --> 00:41:37,850
everybody.

349
00:41:37,850 --> 00:41:42,870
So, we know how to balance it, but we don't yet know how to build this accountability

350
00:41:42,870 --> 00:41:43,870
in.

351
00:41:43,870 --> 00:41:47,970
This is the question of the rest of this century.

352
00:41:47,970 --> 00:41:54,900
It is most acute in this country because we lack the buffers.

353
00:41:54,900 --> 00:42:04,760
If you look at Japan and Germany, the real power is not exercised by stockholders and

354
00:42:04,760 --> 00:42:12,590
not by management, it's exercised by banks, by commercial banks, whose main interest is

355
00:42:12,590 --> 00:42:21,710
not in the dividend or the capital gains, but in the commercial business of that company.

356
00:42:21,710 --> 00:42:28,820
Here is the largest bank of Europe, continental Europe, the Deutsche Bank, and almost more

357
00:42:28,820 --> 00:42:35,640
than one-third of its total revenue comes from one account, its Siemens.

358
00:42:35,640 --> 00:42:41,860
And they directly and indirectly control 70 percent of Siemens stock, but I don't think

359
00:42:41,860 --> 00:42:51,280
they even look at the stock price, because about four-fifths of that income is the commercial

360
00:42:51,280 --> 00:42:54,140
worldwide business of Siemens.

361
00:42:54,140 --> 00:43:02,010
mundane things as letters of credit for exports of x-ray machinery and that then

362
00:43:02,010 --> 00:43:08,380
so the bank has no interest in short-term returns it is concerned with

363
00:43:08,380 --> 00:43:14,320
the market standing and the basic soundness of Siemens and the same is

364
00:43:14,320 --> 00:43:22,290
true in Japan and so these two countries in a way have been not legal legally the

365
00:43:22,290 --> 00:43:29,390
banks don't exist, but an actual constitutional authority there which looks at a business

366
00:43:29,390 --> 00:43:36,870
in terms of its long-term and short-term, of its financial returns and its financial

367
00:43:36,870 --> 00:43:45,680
strength, of its market, because it is, in fact, the first thing a Deutsche Bank looks

368
00:43:45,680 --> 00:43:52,440
at Siemens is market standing, because half of that Siemens profit is financing Siemens

369
00:43:52,440 --> 00:44:01,440
sales financing Siemens export business and so that's where the great bulk of the profit comes

370
00:44:01,440 --> 00:44:08,560
from and so market standing as far as Deutsche Bank and Siemens are concerned are more important

371
00:44:08,560 --> 00:44:17,510
than the profit margin far more and that gives you a balanced view maybe too conservative too long

372
00:44:17,510 --> 00:44:24,790
range. The Japanese and the Germans have rightly been accused of being too much

373
00:44:24,790 --> 00:44:29,970
concerned with the long term. Also, let me say, their shareholders have done much

374
00:44:29,970 --> 00:44:38,750
better than ours, far better, but three times as well. So maybe it isn't quite too

375
00:44:38,750 --> 00:44:49,190
unbalanced. But in this country, where the intermediary is not a commercial banker,

376
00:44:49,190 --> 00:44:57,760
But a short-term speculative investor, we face that problem nakedly.

377
00:44:58,440 --> 00:45:03,530
And in the next month or so, the hostile takeover is out of the way.

378
00:45:04,070 --> 00:45:09,960
And within a short period, it will be something people write PhDs about,

379
00:45:10,560 --> 00:45:14,440
which is always a clear sign that it has nothing more to tell us.

380
00:45:14,440 --> 00:45:21,700
And in the short run, after that, we will tackle the problem.

381
00:45:21,700 --> 00:45:29,940
to whom for what we know the answer to though very few management yet really have equipped

382
00:45:29,940 --> 00:45:36,340
themselves with the instrumentation board the measurements they need they're using them fitfully

383
00:45:37,300 --> 00:45:45,940
but they're learning but to whom and through what agency and there is a grave danger that in the

384
00:45:45,940 --> 00:45:53,690
next crisis whether it's depression or inflation we will see government moving into that spot

385
00:45:53,690 --> 00:46:01,220
as it has in sweden where boards are basically government appointees oh

386
00:46:02,980 --> 00:46:10,060
today in japan there is a struggle for power between the banks if you want to understand

387
00:46:10,060 --> 00:46:16,860
japanese politics today domestic accepted that the only the real issue is one that isn't being

388
00:46:16,860 --> 00:46:23,110
mention it is that for the that
